<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE PUBLISHED-LANE RESTORE CONFIG FOR THE IMAGE BUILD. Selected by `PackageLane=published`, and
  named explicitly by the Dockerfile with -p:RestoreConfigFile=. Nothing else reads it.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The image is the artifact a pipeline signs as provenance, and until now it could
  not carry any. The Dockerfile generated its own config at build time naming exactly two sources,
  /src/DockerPackages and nuget.org - so every first-party package came out of a folder that
  `lz deploycontainer` fills by restoring ON THE HOST. DockerPackageSyncer says so in its own
  summary: "THE CONTAINER'S LANE IS THE WORKSPACE'S LANE, and that is by construction rather than
  by configuration." That is a fine property for a developer loop and a disqualifying one for a
  signed artifact: the bytes depend on the workstation, and the image cannot show which versions
  went in. See DecoupledCd.md section 4.3 - a build record's `builtFrom.lane` exists to express
  exactly this difference, and `Verify` refuses anything but `published`.

  IT NEEDS NO PRIVATE REGISTRY AND NO PAT, which is the fact that makes this cheap. AipHost's
  first-party closure is FIVE `LazyMagic.*` PACKAGES plus twenty-two ProjectReferences (measured
  2026-09-11 from Containers/AipHost/obj/project.assets.json). Every Scutara family - AipApi, the
  *Schema and *Module sets - arrives as a ProjectReference and is COMPILED FROM THE CONTEXT, never
  restored. So the only registry needed is LazyMagicOrg's, whose packages are public, and a job's
  own GITHUB_TOKEN reads those across organisations. This is the same position service-ci is in,
  and service-ci has been green on it with no stored secret since 2026-09-08.

  A CREDENTIAL IS STILL REQUIRED, though the packages are public: GitHub Packages 401s even on a
  public read. It is supplied as a BUILDKIT SECRET, never a build ARG - an ARG is recorded in the
  image history and `docker history` would print it. See the Dockerfile's restore step.

  PREFIXED FILENAME, deliberately, and doing real work here. This file sits in the AipHost PROJECT
  directory, which unlike `.github/` IS on the project's config-discovery chain - so a file named
  `NuGet.Config` here would be picked up by every ordinary `dotnet build` of AipHost on a
  workstation, and its `<clear/>` would silently take that developer off their local lane. The
  prefix is what prevents discovery; the directory cannot.
-->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="LazyMagicRegistry" value="https://nuget.pkg.github.com/LazyMagicOrg/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    <add key="nuget.org"         value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!-- No local folder feed of any kind, which is the whole point of this lane. A missing local
       source is only NU1301 when a requested id maps to it, and none is declared. -->
  <fallbackPackageFolders>
    <clear />
  </fallbackPackageFolders>

  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="LazyMagicRegistry">
      <package pattern="LazyMagic*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
